.TH DUMPFLOPPY 1 "2026-10-06" "dumpfloppy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dumpfloppy \- expose secrets in FAT12, CBMFS, and Amiga ADF images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dumpfloppy
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.IR images ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dumpfloppy
reads a raw IBM PC floppy image
.RI ( .img
or WinImage
.IR .ima ),
an HxC
.I .mfm
bitstream, an 86Box
.I .86f
flux image, a Commodore 1541/1571/1581
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81 ,
a Commodore 1541
.I .g64
or 1571
.I .g71
GCR container, an Amiga
.I .adf ,
or a ZX Spectrum TR-DOS
.I .trd
and prints everything it can prove from the bytes: BIOS boot signature,
jump, OEM name, BIOS Parameter Block, FAT type, both FAT copies, volume
serial and volume label, the full directory (including deleted 8.3
slots), a Type label (DATA until a format matches), XXH64 of each file,
cluster chains, orphans,
and a hex dump of sector 0.
.PP
A whole-image XXH64 catalog names known dumps (title and copy-protection
scheme) when the file hash matches.
A
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81
with a valid CBMFS BAM/header, or a
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
with a valid GCR-1541/GCR-1571 header, is listed as Commodore
(disk name, ID, DOS type, PRG/SEQ directory) instead of inventing a
FAT BPB from C64 bytes.
G64 tracks 1–35 are GCR-decoded onto a 35-track D64 sector map.
G71 tracks are GCR-decoded onto a 70-track D71 sector map.
A DD/HD
.I .adf
with a valid OFS/FFS root is listed as Amiga (volume name, DOS type, directory)
instead of inventing a FAT BPB from Amiga bytes.
A
.I .trd
with a TR-DOS disk-info stamp at logical sector 8 is listed as Spectrum
(disk label, BASIC/CODE directory) instead of inventing a FAT BPB from
160K/320K IBM-sized bytes.
An
.I .ipf
/
.I .woz
/
.I .stx
/
.I .2mg
container is identified (SPS INFO, WOZ INFO, Pasti header, 2IMG prefix)
instead of inventing a FAT BPB from flux bytes.
Pasti
.I .stx
images with standard 512-byte sectors are assembled into a GEMDOS/FAT12
volume for directory listing and extract.
Apple II
.I .woz
5.25-inch tracks are 6-and-2 GCR-decoded into a DOS-order volume and listed
as DOS 3.3 or ProDOS when the catalog is readable.
SPS
.I .ipf
tracks with standard AmigaDOS sectors are assembled into a DD ADF and listed
as OFS/FFS.
A DOS 3.3 VTOC (track 17 sector 0) or ProDOS volume header (block 2)
is listed as Apple (catalog names) from a raw 140K image, a 2IMG payload,
or a decoded WOZ.
A 160K image whose first FAT bytes are
.B FE FF FF
or a 320K image whose first FAT bytes are
.B FF FF FF
is listed as DOS 1.x FAT12 when the boot sector has no BPB and sector 3
is a directory slot.
ImageDisk
.I .imd
files are assembled into that sector image in sector-number order, not
interleave order, and then listed.
A file that starts with
.B IMD
and has no sector payload is reported as ImageDisk with an empty directory.
A boot sector that starts with
.B mov ax,0
followed by
.B mov ds,ax
or
.B mov ss,ax
and has no
.B 55 AA
is a custom booter.
A CP/M 2.2 directory of at least two 32-byte entries is listed by name.
CP/M file bytes are not extracted.
.PP
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-extract
writes recovered files (deleted directory slots and leftover FAT-free/bad
clusters included) into the current directory, or into the directory named
by
.B \-o
when that option is also given.
Unused leftover runs are written as
.B unused_cNNNN.c
/
.B .map
/
.B .txt
/
.B .bin
depending on content (C source, linker memory map, text, or binary).
Text leftovers are split on the DOS Ctrl-Z (`0x1A`) end-of-file byte when
the following bytes look like a new file; a C banner comment supplies the
basename (`unused_SHOWOBJ.c`).
On D64/D71/D81/G64/G71 the host names are the PETSCII listing name plus
.BR .prg / .seq / .usr / .rel / .del .
On FAT12/FAT16,
.B \-x
keeps subdirectory components that come from the directory walk, so
.I RAMTEST/MANUAL.RT
is written as
.I dest/RAMTEST/MANUAL.RT .
A slash or backslash inside one 8.3 or LFN component becomes
.B _
and does not create a host directory.
An 8.3 name
.I A/B.TXT
is one file,
.IR A_B.TXT .
A component longer than 255 bytes is shortened to a 240-byte prefix,
an underscore, and eight hex digits of FNV-1a of that component.
.B \-o
is canonicalized once: a symlink the user named is the destination.
A symlink created under that directory is still skipped.
If one entry cannot be created (a file and directory name clash, or a
path past
.BR PATH_MAX ),
that entry is skipped, later files are still written, and the exit
status is 1.
On ADF, OFS/FFS files are written (directories skipped) with
.B /
in Amiga paths, which stay flattened to
.BR _ .
On TRD, TR-DOS files are written as
.I NAME.C
(deleted
.IR ?AME.C ).
.PP
.I .img
and
.I .ima
are the same on-disk layout (512-byte sectors concatenated).
.B WinImage
uses the
.I .ima
extension; extra bytes past the BPB volume size are reported as a
trailer.
.PP
Deleted directory entries are listed with a
.B ?
in place of the first name byte (on-disk
.BR 0xE5 )
and, unless
.B \-\-no-color
is given, rendered with a light-red background and white bold text.
.PP
Volume serial numbers come from the DOS 4+ extended BPB (signature
.B 0x28
or
.B 0x29
at offset 0x26) and are printed as
.BR XXXX-XXXX .
DOS 3.3 volumes (many 720K game disks) have no serial there; the
root-directory volume label is still reported.
.PP
Options and image paths may appear in any order.
A directory operand expands to
.I *.img ,
.I *.ima ,
.I *.mfm ,
.I *.86f ,
.I *.d64 ,
.I *.d71 ,
.I *.d81 ,
.I *.adf ,
and
.I *.g64 ,
.I *.g71 ,
and
.I *.trd ,
.I *.ipf ,
.I *.woz ,
.I *.stx ,
and
.I *.2mg ,
.I *.dsk ,
and
.I *.po
in that directory (non-recursive).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print
.B dumpfloppy 0.40
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " PATH"
Write the report to
.I PATH
instead of stdout.
If
.I PATH
is a directory (or ends with
.BR / ),
one
.I <stem>.txt
is created per input.
Several inputs with a file
.I PATH
write
.I <stem>_<input-stem>.txt
and warn on stderr.
With
.BR \-x ,
.I PATH
is the extract directory instead of a report path.
It is created if missing.
The default extract directory is the current directory.
If
.I PATH
is an existing regular file,
.B dumpfloppy
exits 1 and does not extract into the current directory.
With
.B \-u
and without
.BR \-x ,
.I PATH
is ignored (the image is rewritten in place) and a warning is printed.
With both
.B \-u
and
.BR \-x ,
.I PATH
is still the extract directory and the image is still rewritten in place.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI colour (default: colour on, including when stdout is not a
TTY).
.TP
.B \-\-no-hex
Skip the 512-byte boot-sector hex dump (default: dump).
.TP
.B \-\-no-deleted
Omit deleted directory entries from the listing (default: show them).
This switch is listing-only:
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-extract
still writes deleted payloads.
.TP
.B \-\-no-unused
Omit leftover FAT-free/bad cluster runs from the listing (default: show them).
This switch is listing-only:
.B \-x
still writes
.B unused_cNNNN
payloads.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON document instead of the text report.
Stdout is that document and nothing else: no ANSI colour, no blank line
between images, no boot-sector hex dump, and no secrets.
.B \-o
keeps the same path rules.
A directory of reports uses
.IR <stem>.json .
Several inputs and one
.B \-o
file use
.IR <outstem>_<instem>.json .
One input and
.B \-o
file writes that exact path.
Stdout is empty when
.B \-o
took the report.
There is no
.B \-\-no-json
switch.
Combined with
.BR \-\-slack ,
.BR \-\-leaked ,
.BR \-\-carve ,
or
.B \-\-sources
the program exits 2 before analysis.
Stderr is
.B "dumpfloppy: --json does not include --slack, --leaked, --carve, or --sources"
and stdout is empty.
Combined with
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-u ,
or
.B \-\-offset
the program exits 2 before analysis.
Stderr is
.B "dumpfloppy: --json does not include -x, -u, or --offset"
and stdout is empty.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract " [" \fIGLOB\fR "]"
Extract files by walking FAT cluster chains,
CBMFS file chains, or Amiga OFS/FFS data blocks.
The directory is
.B .
unless
.BI \-o " DIR"
selects another one (created if missing).
No listing is printed (errors stay on stderr).
Bytes outside printable ASCII are replaced with
.B _
before the file is created.
A slash or backslash that came from the directory walk is a host
directory; one that was inside a single FAT name is also replaced with
.BR _ .
Absolute paths,
.BR . ,
and
.B ..
are still skipped.
A deleted 8.3 name keeps a printable
.B ? .
Deleted 8.3 names and scratched CBM files are included.
With no
.I GLOB
every payload file is written.
.I GLOB
supports
.B *
(any run of characters) and
.B ?
(one character), matched case-insensitively against the 8.3 name.
Quote globs so the shell does not expand them:
.B \-x '*.PKD'
or
.B \-x '5??.PKD' .
.B \-\-extract=\fIGLOB\fR
is unambiguous.
A following
.I .img
/
.I .ima
/
.I .mfm
/
.I .86f
/
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81
/
.I .adf
/
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
path is the floppy image, not a glob.
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-update " FILE"
Overwrite the live file in the image whose name matches
.IR FILE 's
basename (case-insensitive).
Repeatable.
No listing is printed.
On FAT, same size writes the payload onto the existing cluster chain.
A smaller host file frees the unused tail clusters.
A larger host file allocates free clusters, and relocates later live
files when sequential growth needs their clusters.
Both FAT copies and the directory size / first-cluster fields are
updated.
On D64/D71/D81 CBMFS and ADF, only same-size in-place replace is
supported (PRG/SEQ/USR and OFS/FFS files).
Raw
.I .img
/
.I .ima
images are rewritten in place.
HxC
.I .mfm
images patch changed 512-byte DAM payloads (extra HLS IDs are left
untouched).
.I .86f
flux images,
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
GCR images, and CBM REL files are refused.
Glued
.B \-uFILE
is accepted (same as
.B \-u FILE ).
.B \-\-update=\fIFILE\fR
is unambiguous.
.TP
.BI \-\-offset= N
FAT12/FAT16 only.
.I N
is a decimal or
.B 0x
hex byte offset into the logical volume (the image file for a raw
.I .img
/
.I .ima ).
Print one line on stdout: the offset, the sector when the BPB is valid,
either a cluster number or
.BR reserved/FAT/root ,
and the owning file name or one of
.BR free ,
.BR slack ,
or
.BR past-end .
No listing is printed.
Other filesystems write
.B offset map is only implemented for FAT12/FAT16
to stderr and exit 1.
There is no
.B \-\-no-offset
switch; the map is off unless this option is given.
.TP
.B \-\-slack
FAT12/FAT16 only.
For each live payload file, print the unused tail of its last cluster
under
.BR "=== Slack ===" .
Each line is the escaped path
(bytes outside printable ASCII are written as
.BR \exNN ),
the image byte offset of the tail, the length,
and the hex of those bytes only.
Empty tails are omitted.
Clusters on the file chain after the last byte of the file
(ceil of size divided by the cluster size),
and the whole cluster of a zero-length file that owns one, are slack
too.
.B fat_slack_bytes(0)
stays 0; the zero-length case is handled beside that helper.
The listing notes
.B chain longer than size
when the chain extends past the size.
The listing is unchanged when this flag is absent.
Other filesystems still print the normal listing, then write
.B slack is only implemented for FAT12/FAT16
to stderr and exit 1.
There is no
.B \-\-no-slack
switch.
.TP
.B \-\-leaked
FAT12/FAT16 only.
Step by 32 on directory-slot alignment through file slack, free
clusters, and bytes past the end of the filesystem. A cluster region
is aligned to that cluster's file offset. Bytes past the filesystem
are aligned to volume offset 0. A window that starts before the region
or extends past it is not a hit. Slots that belong to a directory
table already walked are skipped.
A hit needs a non-space 8-byte name, a first byte other than 0x00, an
attribute whose bits lie in
.B 0x37
(or attribute
.BR 0x0F ),
a cluster of 0 or in
.BR 2..max ,
and a size no larger than the volume.
Print
.B "=== Leaked directory entries ==="
with offset, escaped name, attribute, cluster, and size.
At most 64 hits, then one stderr warning.
There is no
.B \-\-no-leaked
switch.
Other filesystems still print the normal listing, then write
.B leaked is only implemented for FAT12/FAT16
to stderr and exit 1.
.TP
.B \-\-carve
FAT12/FAT16 only.
Search slack, free clusters, and bytes past the filesystem end
(not live file payload and not directory tables) for
.BR MZ / ZM ,
.BR GIF87a / GIF89a ,
and a printable ASCII run of at least 16 bytes.
Print
.B "=== Carve ==="
with the decimal offset, kind, length, and at most 48 characters of text.
At most 64 hits, then one stderr warning.
There is no
.B \-\-no-carve
switch.
Other filesystems still print the normal listing, then write
.B carve is only implemented for FAT12/FAT16
to stderr and exit 1.
Directory ranges are merged once before the scan.
.TP
.B \-\-sources
FAT12/FAT16 only.
Search the same regions as
.B \-\-carve
(file slack, free clusters, and bytes past the filesystem end).
Live file bytes are not searched.
A match must lie entirely inside one region.
Bytes that overlap a walked directory range are skipped.
Case-sensitive ASCII needles:
.BR "#include" ,
.BR "proc near" ,
.BR "org 100h" ,
and
.BR "uses crt" .
A BASIC line matches at the first byte of a region, or immediately
after CR or LF: one to five decimal digits, one space, then an ASCII
letter (A-Z or a-z).
.B "#INCLUDE"
does not match
.BR "#include" .
Print
.B "=== Source ==="
after the other forensic sections, with the decimal offset, the kind,
and the text.
Kinds are
.BR include ,
.BR proc\-near ,
.BR org\-100h ,
.BR uses\-crt ,
and
.BR basic .
Needle text is the needle.
BASIC text is the digits, the space, and the following printable run,
cut at 48 characters or at CR/LF.
One hit per offset and kind.
At most 64 hits.
The next hit stops the scan and writes
.B Warning: source scan stopped at cap
to stderr.
The exit status stays 0 for that cap.
There is no
.B \-\-no-sources
switch.
Other filesystems still print the normal listing, then write
.B sources is only implemented for FAT12/FAT16
to stderr and exit 1.
No
.B "=== Source ==="
section is printed in that case.
.PP
Directory walk caps are local.
Depth 32 skips only that subdirectory; later siblings and later root
slots are still listed and extracted.
The entry cap of 4096 stops a subdirectory from accepting more names;
root slots that have not been parsed yet are still parsed.
A directory cluster chain stops at 8192 clusters
(chain capped).
A file chain runs until the last valid cluster
(a cycle, a bad cluster, a free cluster, or end of file still stops it)
and is not cut at 8192.
When a cap fires, stderr gets one line that starts with
.B dumpfloppy:
and names the image and the cap kind
(depth, entries, or chain).
The exit status stays 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or help/version requested.
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, unreadable image, or at least one input failed.
.TP
.B 2
.B \-\-json
combined with
.BR \-\-slack ,
.BR \-\-leaked ,
.BR \-\-carve ,
or
.BR \-\-sources ,
or with
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-u ,
or
.BR \-\-offset .
Analysis does not run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a 720K WinImage floppy:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Extract every file, including deleted names, into the current directory:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima \-x
.RE
.PP
Extract into a chosen directory:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy \-x \-o out disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Map byte 2048 of a FAT12 image:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy \-\-offset=2048 disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Show slack tails, leaked directory slots, and carved signatures:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy \-\-slack \-\-leaked \-\-carve disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Search slack, free clusters, and bytes past the filesystem for source text:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy \-\-sources disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Machine-readable report (there is no
.BR \-\-no-json ):
.RS
.B dumpfloppy \-\-json disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
List and extract a Commodore 1541 D64:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.d64
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.d64 \-x
.RE
.PP
List and extract a 1541 G64 or 1571 G71:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g64
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g64 \-x
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g71 \-x
.RE
.PP
List a 1571 D71 or 1581 D81, or extract an Amiga ADF:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk.d71
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy work.adf \-x
.RE
.PP
Extract only
.B .PKD
files:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima \-x '*.PKD'
.RE
.PP
Overwrite
.B PENGUIN.EXE
in a Batman HxC dump after editing the host file:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.mfm \-u PENGUIN.EXE
.RE
.PP
Same update with the glued short option:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.mfm \-uPENGUIN.EXE
.RE
.PP
Plain text, no hex, write beside the image:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy --no-color --no-hex disk1.ima -o disk1.txt
.RE
.PP
Batch a directory of images:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy ./floppies -o ./reports/
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR file (1),
.BR hexdump (1),
.BR mdir (1)
